  "response": "A failure investigation of this kind proceeds by establishing what each diagnostic technique can and cannot resolve, and only then asking which account of the event the whole body of evidence will carry. Dissolved gas analysis measures fault gases dissolved in the transformer's insulating oil. Acetylene in transformer oil is a marker of high-energy electrical arcing. Acetylene is produced in large quantities by normal thermal ageing below 150 degrees Celsius, and such routine low-temperature generation cannot be squared with acetylene's standing as a signature of arcing energy. Carbon dioxide dissolved in the oil is the primary indicator of arcing within the winding, an attribution that likewise rules out acetylene being the marker of high-energy arcing, since the two assignments cannot both hold.\n\nDigital fault recorders capture current and voltage waveforms at sub-millisecond resolution, and historical oil sample trends establish whether gas concentrations rose gradually before the failure; waveform capture at sub-millisecond resolution cannot be squared with the slow history of gas concentrations being settled from archived samples. Vacuum circuit breaker operation can produce steep-fronted restrike overvoltages; as a result, surge counters fitted to lightning arresters record the number of surge discharges. The waveform record resolved to fractions of a millisecond is precisely what shows that a restriking vacuum interrupter imposes steep-fronted overvoltages on the transformer terminals, since nothing coarser would capture a front of that duration. Whereas the trend running through successive archived oil samples speaks to accumulation over months, the arrester's surge counter registers nothing but a tally of discrete discharge events.\n\nFuran analysis of the oil estimates the degree of polymerisation of the paper insulation. Dielectric dissipation factor measurement assesses the condition of the insulation system. Applied to the failed unit, the furan determination resulted in the winding paper sample returning a degree of polymerisation of 180; in other words, the average cellulose chain in the winding paper sample contained 180 glucose units. Whereas the surge counter reading can at best establish how often the arrester has conducted, a figure of 180 fixes the mechanical fragility of the paper at the moment the unit failed.\n\nSweep frequency response analysis detects winding displacement and mechanical deformation. Internal inspection after de-tanking locates the physical failure site on the windings. The investigator's task is then one of sequencing: the electrical records fix the instant and shape of the disturbance, the chemical records fix the condition of the insulation that received it, and the mechanical findings fix where the dielectric finally gave way. An internal origin is sustained where degraded insulation is already evident and no external disturbance of adequate severity appears on record; an external origin is sustained where a severe terminal transient is documented and the solid insulation is otherwise in sound order. Where the two bodies of evidence remain opposed, the discrepancy is itself the finding to be explained, and the work proceeds by identifying which instrument's scope has been exceeded.",
